the faces of 43 student teachers who  disappeared in 2014.  first they were ambushed by police  then kidnapped and handed over to cartel  gunman  in what's become one of the worst human  rights scandals in decades  the initial investigation was led by  this man former mexican attorney general  jesus murillo karam  this week he was arrested on charges of  forced disappearance torture and  obstruction of justice  the disappearance of 43 students on the  night of the 26th to the 27th of  september 2014  constituted a state crime in which  members of the guerrero anidos criminal  group and agents of various mexican  state institutions were involved in  which federal and state authorities at  the highest level were omissive and  negligent  warrants were also issued for 83 local  officials police officers and army  soldiers  vindication for family members who never  believed mirullo karan's version of  events  but with proof the moment we as parents  are sure what the attorney general is  saying is the truth only then will we  accept it  but the motive behind what happened is  still up for debate  what happened was these mexican students  they they basically commandeered a bus  and it looks like somehow they they  commandeered the wrong bus this may have  there's a theory that maybe there were  drugs on board that this somehow  belonged to one of the drug cartels and  we know unfortunately about all of these  nexuses between  the mexican state and drug cartels in a  drug war or a drug related conflict that  has led to more than a hundred thousand  disappearances  over the last 12 years in mexico  despite the arrests the remains of only  three students have been found and  identified  and whilst family members remain hopeful  investigators say that there is no  evidence to suggest the students are  still alive  shimon freeman powell sky news
 

